# Ship feature work to main

When a **major task is clearly finished** — or the user has moved on to a different product area — do not leave the work only on a local feature branch. Land it on `main` so Vercel and the repo stay current.

## When this applies

Trigger automatically when any of these is true:

- The user says the feature/prompt/pass is done, or asks to ship / PR / merge
- You completed the stated goal and they start an unrelated request
- The conversation is wrapping up after substantial app changes (new routes, UX pass, data model, etc.)

Do **not** trigger mid-implementation, for tiny typo-only edits, or when they only asked a question.

## Required flow

Stay on the correct `feature/<area>-<short-goal>` branch (see feature-branch rule). Then:

1. **Commit** pending work if needed (concise why-focused message; never commit secrets)
2. **Push** the feature branch (`git push -u origin HEAD`)
3. **Open a PR into `main`** with `gh pr create`:
   - **Title**: short and specific (e.g. `Scale Studio: dedicated scale workspaces`)
   - **Body**: 2–4 bullets — what changed and why; include a brief test plan
4. **Merge when safe**:
   - If the PR is mergeable (no conflicts) and checks are green or not required, merge with `gh pr merge --merge` (or `--squash` if the repo prefers squash)
   - If there are conflicts, failing checks, or review blockers: leave the PR open, report the URL and blockers — do **not** force-merge
5. **Sync local**: `git checkout main && git pull`, then delete the merged feature branch locally/remotely when clean

## PR quality bar

- One feature / one concern per PR (do not dump unrelated WIP)
- Title readable in the Vercel / GitHub feed
- No “WIP”, “stuff”, or vague titles
- Return the PR URL (and merge status) to the user

## Safety

- Never force-push `main`
- Never `--no-verify` unless the user explicitly asks
- If uncommitted work spans multiple unrelated features, split or ask before shipping
